-attachment uses the **imported Model Pivot basis**, not raw MeshPart axes. During Studio import the bridge preserves both the pivot position and its orientation in `Game1WeaponGrip` (the `Model.WorldPivot` property does not replicate on its own). At runtime the server rebuilds that grip from the template's current Model Pivot rotation while retaining the existing authored grip point. Both server equip and client render align the resulting grip directly to the animated hand socket, without a hard-coded -90° correction.
-
-This is a relative frame contract: `grip.CFrame = referencePart.CFrame:ToObjectSpace(CFrame.new(gripPoint) * model:GetPivot().Rotation)`. For FBX files with different Blender→Roblox conversions (e.g. a dagger with +90° X PivotOffset, and a sword with identity PivotOffset), the same target socket orientation produces the same visual weapon basis. No per-slug angles are needed. The runtime contract is `runtime-pivot-relative-grip-v3`; old templates containing position-only grips are upgraded at every equip without reimport or republish. The one grip solver also applies to NPC equipment and retains the authored OBB for the weapon attack volume. Studio Play must verify geometry-facing direction and active hit volumes after this change.
+attachment is runtime-canonical. on every equip the server discards any stale `Game1WeaponGrip` orientation from the Studio template (or creates the grip if an old template has none), preserves only its authored grip point, and rebuilds one `Game1WeaponGrip` with canonical world XYZ axes. every weapon therefore uses one solver and never falls back to `Model:PivotTo(socketFrame)`. the target weapon socket is rotated -90 degrees around its local X axis before `desiredSocket * currentGrip^-1` alignment. melee uses the right weapon socket and bow uses the left weapon socket. this global rule is independent of per-asset pivot orientation and cannot accumulate rotation per frame.
